{"answer":"There are 7 subsets:\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} has sum 9. Exhaustiveness follows by partitioning every requested nonempty subset according to its unique largest element m. The cases m=4,5,6,7,8 are disjoint and cover all possibilities; their residual-sum counts are 1,2,2,1,1, respectively.","approaches":[{"description":"Partition the requested subsets by their unique largest element, then enumerate the required residual sum using smaller elements.","id":"largest-element-cases","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It proves both coverage and disjointness while reducing the enumeration to five small cases."},{"description":"Present an unstructured list of candidate subsets.","id":"ungrouped-listing","outcome":"rejected","reason":"Without an exclusion or partition argument, a bare list does not establish exhaustiveness."}],"change_log":["Retained the largest-element proof and made the lower and upper bounds on the largest element explicit.","Retained explicit residual enumerations for all five largest-element cases.","Made the coverage and no-duplicate argument explicit through the unique-largest-element partition.","No audit objection remains unresolved; no computational checks are proposed."],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks: \"How many subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9?\"","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"target-family","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Let F be the family of subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the largest-element range, the five residual enumerations, and their partition.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["target-family"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The members of F are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["largest-range","case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","partition"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The disjoint case counts are summed in total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["total-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Verified directly in example.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a requested subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["example"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The unique-largest-element partition is complete and disjoint, and each case is completely enumerated.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"exhaustiveness","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The displayed list is exhaustive and contains no duplicates.","step_ids":["partition"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"largest-range","justification":"A member of F has sum 9 and is therefore nonempty, so it has a largest element m. If m<=3, all its elements lie in {1,2,3}, whose total is 6<9. Since m belongs to the stated ground set, m<=8.","statement":"For every member of F, its largest element m lies in {4,5,6,7,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-eight","justification":"After including 8, the smaller elements must sum to 1. Among positive distinct integers below 8, only {1} has sum 1.","statement":"The m=8 case contributes exactly {1,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-seven","justification":"After including 7, the smaller elements must sum to 2. Among positive distinct integers below 7, only {2} has sum 2.","statement":"The m=7 case contributes exactly {2,7}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-six","justification":"After including 6, the smaller elements must sum to 3. The only subsets of {1,2,3,4,5} summing to 3 are {3} and {1,2}.","statement":"The m=6 case contributes exactly {3,6} and {1,2,6}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-five","justification":"After including 5, the smaller elements must sum to 4. The only subsets of {1,2,3,4} summing to 4 are {4} and {1,3}.","statement":"The m=5 case contributes exactly {4,5} and {1,3,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range"],"id":"case-four","justification":"After including 4, the smaller elements must sum to 5. The subsets of {1,2,3} have sums 0,1,2,3,3,4,5,6, so only {2,3} sums to 5.","statement":"The m=4 case contributes exactly {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-range","case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four"],"id":"partition","justification":"Every nonempty finite subset has exactly one largest element. By largest-range that element is one of 4 through 8, so the five cases cover F; different largest elements make the cases disjoint. The five preceding steps completely enumerate their respective cases.","statement":"The five cases form a complete, disjoint partition of F."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","partition"],"id":"total-count","justification":"The complete disjoint cases have counts 1, 1, 2, 2, and 1. Their sum is 7.","statement":"|F|=1+1+2+2+1=7."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"example","justification":"Both 1 and 8 belong to the stated ground set, and 1+8=9.","statement":"{1,8} belongs to F."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
